  "name": "Self-balancing two-wheel robot",
  "summary": "An inverted pendulum on two wheels: an ESP32 reads an MPU-6050 200 times a second and drives two Nema17 steppers through A4988 drivers to stay upright, on a 3S 18650 pack carried high on the top deck.",
  "spec": {
    "name": "Self-Balancing Two-Wheel Robot",
    "function": "A two-wheeled robot that balances like an inverted pendulum: an ESP32 runs a PID loop on the MPU-6050 tilt and drives two Nema17 steppers through A4988 drivers, with the 3S 18650 pack on the top deck and a 5 V step-down for the electronics.",
    "quote": "Let's do a self-balancing robot, the classic two-wheeler. Two Nema17 steppers with A4988 drivers, an ESP32 and an MPU-6050, three 18650s on top. To catch a 12 degree lean each wheel has to shove half the robot back under its centre of mass, and a stepper loses torque at speed, so give the motors three times what that takes. The top deck can't sag more than 0.2 millimetres under the battery. It should balance two hours, cost under 90 euros and weigh under 1.5 kilos.",
